kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-9) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Actually ship the 0.1.0-8 postinst. It never reached the package: the build sync copied a
    named list of files (changelog, the two python services, the release tarball) and
    debian/postinst was not on it, so 0.1.0-8 installed without it and the upgrade still did
    not redeploy the release. The sync now copies the whole debian/ directory, and the package
    was checked for the script before publishing this time.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:45:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Deploy the release on upgrade. The package ships a tarball, and unpacking it is done by
    kubeos-dashboard-install.service (kubeos-cli) - Type=oneshot with RemainAfterExit=yes, so
    that unit answers "active (exited)" for ever and a bare `systemctl start` is a no-op.
    Measured 2026-11-20: `apt upgrade kubeos-dashboard` moved the package from 0.1.0-6 to
    0.1.0-7 while the RUNNING console kept the old code, so the fix inside it did nothing until
    the unit was stopped, reset-failed and started by hand. postinst now does exactly that
    (warn-only if it cannot), so an upgrade takes effect immediately.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:35:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Deleting an etcd snapshot from the dashboard now works in a browser. The page issues the
    delete as HTTP DELETE, but the console's CORS preflight advertised only
    "POST, PUT, GET, OPTIONS" - so the browser refused to send the request and the page showed
    "Failed to fetch", with nothing reaching the service at all (measured 2026-11-20: the
    console journal had no DELETE from the browser, while the same call from curl - which
    ignores CORS - deleted the file perfectly). DELETE is now in the advertised list.

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 20:25:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Stop printing a traceback every time a browser leaves. Neither `authproxy.py` nor
    `console.py` overrode `BaseServer.handle_error`, and that default prints a full stack for
    ANY exception that escapes a request - including the entirely normal `BrokenPipeError`
    from a closed tab, a reload mid-page, or a probe that gave up. Measured 2026-11-20 on the
    busiest node: 12 tracebacks in one journal, every one raised inside `socketserver.write`,
    each of them making a healthy gateway look like it was crashing. Both services now answer
    such a disconnect with one journal line ("went away mid-response") and keep the traceback
    for anything that is genuinely broken.
  * The vendored release was rebuilt from this tree and compared file by file against the one
    that shipped in 0.1.0-5: every file is byte-identical except `authproxy.py` and
    `console/console.py` (plus the generated `VERSION` build-date and `sha256sums.txt`).

 -- KubeOS <mail@kubeos.in>  Fri, 20 Nov 2026 07:58:00 +0530

kubeos-dashboard (0.1.0-5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Ship the release archive itself instead of rebuilding it. The package carries the exact
    tarball that was verified in the ISO (vendor/release, its sha256 checked at build time),
    so package content and tested artefact are the same bytes by construction. Rebuilding
    from source meant trusting a branch, and doing that shipped main - which is behind dev -
    dropping the rebalance feature and the etcd-snapshot fix.
  * Version numbering follows Debian exactly from here: upstream version 0.1.0, revision 5
    (the fifth packaging revision of this upstream version).

